value: add a scalar fast path to list intersections

Every entry of a list intersection funnels through
gst_value_intersect(), paying two G_IS_VALUE type checks, the list-type
checks, a compare-function hash lookup and dispatch per entry. For the
dominant caps content (string format lists, int rate lists) the whole
intersection of two same-type scalars is a single equality check:
no same-type intersect function exists for these types, so compare
equal means copy, compare unequal means empty.

Handle G_TYPE_STRING and G_TYPE_INT entry pairs directly in
gst_value_intersect_list() and both gst_value_intersect_list_list()
loops, with NULL string handling mirroring gst_value_compare_string().
Everything else falls back to the generic path unchanged.

gstcapsops benchmark, best of 5, together with the preceding
strict-equality commit:

  value_intersect string x 82-list   2510 ->   312 ns  (8.0x)
  value_intersect 82-list x 82-list 10873 ->  5123 ns  (2.1x)
  intersect fixed x template         2600 ->   993 ns  (2.6x)
  can_intersect fixed x template      846 ->   326 ns  (2.6x)
  intersect template x template     16091 -> 11865 ns  (1.4x)
  is_equal template x copy          13351 ->  5873 ns  (2.3x)
  8-hop query fold (template)      197476 -> 97207 ns  (2.0x)

The remaining template x template cost is dest building (a string copy
per matched entry), not the search.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

